"A replication topology identifies authoritative writers, replicas, and the paths that copy state between failure domains.\n\nUse when: Review replication ownership and failover prerequisites. Explain synchronous and asynchronous replica relationships.\n\nAvoid when: Exact consensus messages or query execution operators are required.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote database or service replicas with explicit roles. Arrows encode replication direction and mode. Padded boundaries identify regions, zones, or other failure domains.\n\nBuild: Choose one environment and label its observed or proposed status. Identify writers, readers, and replica membership. Add replication modes, promotion rules, and conflict handling where applicable. Place failure domains side by side and route inter-domain links through clear boundary gaps.\n\nCheck: Write authority is unambiguous in normal operation. Asynchronous copying is not described as zero-loss by default. Multiwriter conflict rules and promotion prerequisites are documented. Role labels and domain titles are clear of replication arrows.\n\nPitfalls: Inferring automatic failover from the presence of a replica. Using bidirectional arrows without explaining conflict resolution.\n\nExample: A primary replicates synchronously within one zone pair and asynchronously to a recovery region.\n\nAlternatives: Consensus protocol diagram, Failover topology."
